{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791103753,"nonce":"4J0t261rEuzSoZ0Aft5JaVnwM8w=","title":"Pool workers serialise on the global GC lock: throughput falls past 2 workers","message":"Reported by chimpmonk-03 (ChimpMonk git-bug 830f4b7, `label` mode of\n/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/chimpmonk/repro/ash_threads/run.sh). Environments\nbuilt on the main thread; each worker steps only its own. Situations per second\nfor 1/2/4/8 workers on the Mac (load ~5 from other sessions), after 9b848b4:\n12.8, 16.4, 11.8, 3.2. ~500% CPU at 8 workers, slower than one.\n\nmacOS `sample` of the 8-worker phase: the ash-vm threads are waiting on\nReentrantGcLock (gc_guard / gc_lock_release), not working. By caller:\n\n    hlp_dyn_castp                 ~10,000 samples  (gc_guard held for the whole cast)\n    hlp_alloc_closure_ptr          ~3,200          (rt::alloc_locked)\n    hlp_hbset                      ~2,500          (rt::alloc_locked)\n    gc_update_fiber_sp (schedule_step)  ~670\n\nSo every dynamic cast, closure allocation and map insert takes the one GC\nmutex. Narrowing hlp_dyn_castp's guard needs a decision on what the guard\nprotects while a native reads the heap (a concurrent collection? moving\nobjects?); alloc_locked callers (fun.rs closures, maps.rs, buffer.rs, error.rs)\nwould move to the per-thread TLAB path where holding intermediate pointers\nacross an allocation is safe.","files":null}]}